    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_ff3f72b249e2be69d9ac1f45190296cc19d40151a769239ab35d99ea45667e9c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The SO parity distinction is exposed analytically in the large-weight off-diagonal calculation. Petersson averaging and the parity-restricted Bessel sum lead to Kloosterman sums; character orthogonality and Dirichlet GRH make nonprincipal characters lower order, while the principal character leaves the |xi|>=1 Fourier-tail term with opposite signs for weights congruent to 0 and 2 modulo 4. The shared diagonal is therefore refined into SO(even) and SO(odd) densities on support (-2,2), after replacing the fixed K-scale statistic by the true conductor-scaled one. This gain is conditional and specific to this parity-restricted large-weight interface.",
      "parent_sources": [
